  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 246 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nDeep moisture from the Gulf will remain in place across the forecast \narea and much of the Southeast as the long term period begins on \nSaturday. As the axis of a mid level trough moves across the \nAppalachians, it will continue to nudge a weakening frontal boundary \nthrough the far northern tier. Scattered showers and thunderstorms \nwill continue in portions of north and west Georgia in the vicinity \nof this front, though coverage will be far less widespread then \nSaturday afternoon with the loss of daytime instability. Low \ntemperatures on Sunday morning will range from the upper 60s to low \n70s, which will be very close to dewpoints. With light winds and a \nnearly saturated airmass, areas of fog will be possible as the \nmorning begins. During the daytime, the aforementioned trough (and a \ndeveloping surface low) will lift northeast into the Atlantic, \ncausing the frontal boundary to lose support and stall across \nnorthern Georgia, where it will eventually wash out.\n\nScattered afternoon pulse thunderstorms are expected on Sunday, \nthough there will be a local maximum (40-50% chance) in coverage \ncloser to where the stalled boundary ends up. Organized severe \nweather is not expected given a lack of shear and deep moisture \nthrough the atmospheric column, though ample diurnal instability \nwill allow for a few storms to become strong. Such storms could \nproduce gusty winds, frequent lightning, and bursts of heavy rain. \nWith lesser precipitation and cloud coverage on Sunday compared to \nSaturday, temperatures are expected to rebound by a couple of \ndegrees, warming into the low to mid 90s in all but the far \nnortheastern mountains. With dewpoints remaining in the 70s, heat \nindex values will climb into the low to mid 100s in areas south of I-\n85 and in the upper 90s in areas to the north in the peak heating \nhours. \n\nThere will be little change in the airmass during the early parts of \nnext week, with humid conditions and above normal temperatures \ncombining for heat indices exceeding 100 across the majority of the \narea Monday through Wednesday. A ridge moving across the eastern \nCONUS on Monday will help suppress convection across the majority of \nthe area, with only isolated afternoon thunderstorms currently \nforecast in areas of south-central Georgia. The axis of the ridge \nwill begin to shift northeast of the forecast area on Tuesday into \nWednesday, leading to a slight rebound in thunderstorm chances. In a \nmoist and unstable airmass but in the absence of any surface forcing \nmechanisms, isolated to widely scattered thunderstorms are expected \non Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons.",
